  "transcript": "# Proposals for backbench debates\n\n## Homeowners targeted over restrictive covenants\n> A company called Asset Invest has been buying restrictive covenants and demanding large payments from homeowners when they try to sell, and MPs want a Westminster Hall debate to raise the issue nationally and feed into leasehold reform.\n\nChair: Marie, please present your case for a Westminster Hall debate on protection for homeowners targeted by Asset Invest.\nMarie Goldman: In my Chelmsford constituency residents have been contacted by Asset Invest, which bought restrictive covenants in titles and is demanding tens of thousands of pounds when people try to sell.\nMarie Goldman: People discover this when their property goes on Rightmove or when they're in probate; some feel pressured and have paid over £20,000 to avoid losing a sale.\nDr Keiran Mullan: At least 15 MPs have constituents affected; Asset Invest holds covenants in many more constituencies. Examples: one Gosport constituent paid £11,300, Stafford £7,500, and Fareham & Waterlooville over £10,000.\nDr Keiran Mullan: We met surveyors, lenders and solicitors and all agree it's a problem. A debate now would help set out the background before the Commonhold and Leasehold Reform Bill.\nMary Glindon: The Committee asks for an equal split of Government and Opposition speakers; your application names only one Government Back Bencher. Can you find three more?\nDr Keiran Mullan: Government MPs are often PPSs and hesitant to sign quickly. We can send a list of affected Government constituencies but the form asks for named supporters—so we'll approach them.\nMr Dillon: You said 15 MPs affected and then another 41 seats—does that mean about 56 constituencies in total?\nDr Keiran Mullan: At least. We've only tracked one housebuilder whose covenants were bought for £1, and we've since identified others, so the true number could be higher.\nChair: How is Asset Invest identifying who benefits from covenants?\nDr Keiran Mullan: They've legally bought the covenant rights, so on paper they 'have the benefit'. They don't maintain or enforce standards—it's a revenue-seeking practice rather than a genuine planning or maintenance concern.\nMarie Goldman: They seem to make the sums up and use pressure selling. Constituents pay because they'd rather lose money than risk a house sale collapsing.\nChair: If you can supply four Government MPs who will actually speak in the debate (an even split of eight speakers), the Committee will support the application. Please send those names to the Clerks.\nDr Keiran Mullan: We'll approach the MPs and provide names. Thank you.\n\n\n## Protecting the next generation's freedom of religion or belief\n> An MP requested a 90-minute Westminster Hall debate on protecting children’s freedom of religion or belief overseas, highlighting forced conversions and asking for FCDO engagement.\n\nChair: Next application: a debate on protecting the rights of the next generation to freedom of religion or belief. Jim, is this for a Tuesday Westminster Hall 90-minute slot?\nJim Shannon: Yes — I'm happy with Tuesday or Thursday. I'm chair of the APPG for international freedom of religion or belief. The focus is specifically on children and young people facing persecution, forced conversions and restrictions.\nJim Shannon: Freedom of religion or belief is a fundamental right (article 18 of the Universal Declaration and article 14 of the UN Convention on the Rights of the Child). Parliament has shown sustained cross-party interest.\nJim Shannon: We have had a special envoy and recent parliamentary scrutiny of forced conversions, especially in Pakistan, and I want time to put the next generation's needs on record and press for action.\nMr Dillon: Which Government Department should respond?\nJim Shannon: This is mainly an FCDO matter. I think Chris Elmore or Ministers covering the Far East would be appropriate given where forced conversions are being reported.\nMr Dillon: A practical point: your form was out of date and didn't ask which Department answers. The Clerks have sent you an updated form for future applications.\nJonathan Davies: I see Ruth Jones has been added to your backers, so the application now meets the Committee's composition rules.\nChair: Thanks — the Clerks will be in touch to arrange the slot and ensure the right Department is invited. There's also a separate letter on Bangladesh moving through the office.\nJim Shannon: Thank you. I appreciate the Committee's help.\n\n\n## Debate to educate on harmful breeding of pets\n> An MP sought an educational Westminster Hall debate to highlight the suffering caused by breeding cats and dogs for exaggerated features and the strain this places on rescues.\n\nChair: Ruth, please present your application on breeding dogs and cats with exaggerated features for a Westminster Hall debate.\nRuth Jones: This should be an educational debate. Breeding animals with flat faces, floppy ears or twisted tails causes pain, breathing problems and shortened lives, yet they're still promoted as cute.\nRuth Jones: Social media and celebrity culture encourage demand. People pester parents for these pets then discover they need expensive surgery—sometimes £6,000—so rescues are left to pick up the cost.\nAlison Hume: There is a risk that emphasising suffering can encourage 'rescue' motivations rather than reducing demand. Is there any urgency about timing?\nRuth Jones: No specific timing. The goal is to raise public awareness so people think twice before buying and to reduce the pressure on rescue centres.\nChair: Thank you. The Clerks will be in touch about arranging the debate and speakers.\nRuth Jones: Thanks very much.\n",
